WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.897 --> 00:00:06.230
SHERICE TORRES: Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:07.690
My name is Sherice Torres.

00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:09.790
I'm the Global Director
of Inclusion at Google,

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.520
and I'm excited to partner
with my colleague Ruby

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.030
Chang from the GCP
K-12 Education team

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:19.450
to speak with you about the
role of Employee Resource Groups

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:22.570
in building a culture of
inclusion and belonging.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:25.420
Ruby will kick off our time
together with a deep dive

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.810
into the
Women@GoogleCloud group,

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.880
and I'll give an overview
of Google's overall approach

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.770
to Employee Resource Groups.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.020
Over to you, Ruby.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.360
RUBY CHANG: Thank you, Sherice.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.390
Hi, everyone.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.112
I'm Ruby Chang.

00:00:38.112 --> 00:00:39.945
I serve as one of the
global leads for Women

00:00:39.945 --> 00:00:42.030
at Google Cloud,
and I also serve

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:45.000
as a Google Cloud Platform
for K-12 Education

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.520
lead across North America.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.910
Our chapter of Women@ is a
global network of about 850

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.250
women and allies that are
passionate about building

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.370
a diverse and inclusive culture
where all of us can thrive

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.380
and bring our whole
selves to work.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.950
More broadly, Women@ is
the largest ERG at Google,

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:06.880
with over 120 chapters
at 52 offices globally.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.740
Our start was very grassroots.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.350
Back when we were a small
team of what was then

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:13.540
Google Enterprise,
three women just

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.950
wanted to connect with
others across their team.

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:17.920
And even without a
designated leader,

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.120
they had a desire to
get to know one another.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.990
So they just got started
with video meetings, which

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:26.140
eventually became face-to-face
lunches as the team grew,

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.690
and that eventually evolved
to the large-scale programming

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:30.960
that we're at today.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.780
It has been incredibly powerful
to see this community grow

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.110
into a global network.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.040
And looking back at our humble
roots and successful years

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.960
since then, we've
also seen that there

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.530
are times when we needed a
lot of help, when we needed

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.500
to seek out executive
sponsorship,

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.250
and times when we
couldn't find any.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.710
What has really helped
us through all of this

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.460
is staying centered
on a mission.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:55.140
We're a global network focused
on empowering, connecting,

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.810
developing, and retaining
talent within Google Cloud.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.110
This is not just important
for the Cloud team,

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:05.480
but it's also aligned to
Google's ERG and Women@ goals.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:08.130
We have a committed leadership
team that meets weekly,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.610
and we get together twice
a year for an off-site.

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:13.590
That keeps us true and
focused to our mission.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.910
We use that time to evaluate
the past few months,

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.490
look at the feedback, and
plan events and avenues

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.965
to bring the full
community together again.

00:02:21.965 --> 00:02:24.590
You'd be correct to imagine that
our professional relationships

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:26.570
very quickly become
very personal ones,

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:29.130
as we dedicate time to get
to know one another better

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.580
and tap into each
other's strengths.

00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:33.590
We have no budget,
so we rely entirely

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:36.380
on our commitment, our
creativity, tenacity,

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.057
and passion to get things done.

00:02:39.057 --> 00:02:41.640
We have three pillars that guide
Women@GoogleCloud and connect

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.920
back to the heart of
everything that we do.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:46.780
And as I share these pillars
and some examples with you,

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:48.572
I also want to share
some things that we've

00:02:48.572 --> 00:02:50.140
learned along the way.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.810
Our first pillar is
to raise awareness.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.510
This speaks to raising awareness
around gender diversity

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.920
as well as promoting
talent to join and develop

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.198
with Google Cloud teams.

00:03:00.198 --> 00:03:02.490
For example, we maintain a
[? cadence ?] with the Cloud

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.080
staffing teams and
check in quarterly,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.150
and we have a dialogue
on how our community can

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:07.750
support their goals.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.470
We want to be intentional
about creating connections

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:14.280
and host external recruiting
events as one avenue to do so.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.720
That encourages industry talent
to consider joining our team.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.480
I mentioned earlier the idea
of an executive sponsor.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.730
At one point, we just
didn't have women leaders

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:25.410
to take this on with us.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:28.495
But executive leadership
is crucial to ERG success,

00:03:28.495 --> 00:03:31.470
and we're so lucky to have
Nina Harding, Google Cloud's

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:35.570
Chief of Global Partner Programs
and Strategy, to take this on.

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:37.650
And her sponsorship
and advocacy are

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.260
key to elevating the impact
of our women and allies

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.230
at the leadership level.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.640
Our second pillar is
building the community.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.040
It challenges us to
really consider, how do we

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.260
create a culture that
helps retain women talent

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:53.020
and is inclusive of
allies, especially now,

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.040
in a digital environment?

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.580
This is not something that
we can do just once a year,

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:01.390
checking off a box with
one event or a big idea.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:03.810
We approach this as a
series of experiences,

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.550
and it requires a lot of
communication and activation

00:04:06.550 --> 00:04:09.240
across our entire global team.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:12.000
Let me take you
through one decision.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.250
A way to represent
our unique culture

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:17.649
is with the brand or logo that
represents what we stand for,

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:20.370
so we embarked on launching
a fun logo contest.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.790
We collected
submissions, held a vote,

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:24.480
and our finalist
was a great graphic

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:27.570
of a woman avatar with a
superhero cape trailing

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.110
behind her.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:30.540
We loved it.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:32.520
But we also started
hearing from our community

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.800
that perhaps this was not
the most representative icon

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:37.410
to represent all women in Cloud.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.260
The avatar was slim,
had a lighter skin tone,

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:41.550
and was able-bodied.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.170
And we took that
feedback very seriously.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.590
So we opted, instead,
for a more inclusive

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:49.850
colorful text-based
logo that we have today,

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:53.250
and it was only second
by a few votes anyhow.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:54.810
Outside of creative
choices, there

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.970
are a lot of logistical
decisions for a community

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.580
of hundreds of members.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.020
So to scale, we have to be
intentional about aligning

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:03.120
on our communication channels.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.950
We primarily use
Google Groups, which

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:07.110
functions both as our
message board as well

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.640
as our email distribution.

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.770
And this group is
intentionally created

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.930
to be a public and
not private group,

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.810
so that all Googlers
can read and engage.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.180
It allows us to maintain an
open culture and approach

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.630
to events and discussion.

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:23.060
We also have an
internal web page

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:25.850
that serves as a home base
for upcoming activities,

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:29.840
past archived events, and more.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.530
There is a unique challenge
when your team grows globally.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:34.160
And in order for
that to be effective,

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:37.940
we really need to think global
and empower folks to activate

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:39.800
and organize locally.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.440
We have an incredible network
of ambassadors who help lead

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:44.960
the Women@ presence in their
local home offices as part

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.197
of this effort.

00:05:47.197 --> 00:05:49.030
We're also very proud
of our recently formed

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:51.580
regional chapters
in EMEA and APAC

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:54.890
and functional chapters that are
organizational or team-based,

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.190
like Women@Coreinfrastructure.

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:58.930
We tap into each
other's platforms

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:04.430
and connect as a stronger global
network of women and allies.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.410
We also want to
recognize that community

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:08.327
is inside and outside.

00:06:08.327 --> 00:06:10.160
There needs to be a
balance between the work

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:12.890
that we do here at Google
and how we share and invite

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.710
other leaders to join
us in that mission.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:18.530
Last year, we partnered with
the Kellogg School of Management

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:20.570
to launch a global
speaker series,

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:22.175
where we brought
their professors who

00:06:22.175 --> 00:06:24.080
were experts in the
field to deliver

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.720
sprints of professional
development topics.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:29.520
We had about 200 members
attend each talk,

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:32.280
and it sparked internal
dialogue with one another,

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:36.770
in addition to offering external
networking opportunities.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.950
Our third and last pillar
is empower and develop.

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:41.780
This is centered on
providing opportunities

00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:43.850
for professional
development and training,

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:45.740
as well as supporting
the promotion of women

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:48.440
to leadership positions.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.870
It is so important to create
a culture of celebration

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.540
and recognition for the growth
and accomplishments of what

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:54.860
we do.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:58.280
Our members every day go
well beyond just our teams

00:06:58.280 --> 00:06:59.600
or our customers.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:01.820
We have an opportunity
to share knowledge, learn

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:04.190
from one another, and
create stronger impact when

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:05.630
we act together.

00:07:05.630 --> 00:07:08.030
So every quarter, we highlight
the awesome work happening

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:11.030
around the world in a newsletter
and encourage recognition

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.000
across teams into our
senior leadership.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.640
This is another place where
executive sponsorship really

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:17.770
comes in.

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:20.660
We want to lift,
amplify, and build trust

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:23.170
across Google Cloud.

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:26.230
If there's one lesson
we've picked up since 2014,

00:07:26.230 --> 00:07:28.840
it's that we're always
learning, we're always growing,

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:32.900
and it never hurts to have
some fun along the way.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:35.750
Looking ahead, while we're all
navigating the remote working

00:07:35.750 --> 00:07:38.600
situation, we especially
want to lean into listening

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:40.280
to our community.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.430
In March, we took steps to
re-evaluate all our 2020

00:07:43.430 --> 00:07:47.950
programming, thinking should
we scrap and replan our events?

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:50.810
And if that occurred, why
don't we just ask our folks?

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:52.730
What's important
to Googlers now?

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:56.210
How do we support our women and
allies doing work from home?

00:07:56.210 --> 00:07:59.390
After that poll, we heard
that our priority really

00:07:59.390 --> 00:08:01.287
fell into three-fold.

00:08:01.287 --> 00:08:03.370
One, to have a better
understanding of the company

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:06.510
resources available to
them during COVID-19.

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:08.390
So we hosted an
open Q&amp;A dialogue

00:08:08.390 --> 00:08:11.030
with our partners on the
People Operations team

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:13.100
to promote discussion
and information sharing

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:15.320
for the unique situations
that a lot of our members

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.450
are actively managing today.

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:20.960
Second, Googlers want to spend
more time with Cloud leaders,

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:23.240
so we launched Candid
Cloud, and opened discussion

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:25.820
with two regional Cloud
leaders every month.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:27.950
And, third, Googlers
don't want to slow down

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:30.020
when it comes to internal
and external networking

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:31.350
opportunities.

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:33.679
So we always ask ourselves,
how can we integrate

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:36.059
this in everything that we do?

00:08:36.059 --> 00:08:38.520
One thing we did learn that
folks really don't want right

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:40.559
now is another group chat.

00:08:40.559 --> 00:08:44.340
It seems that a lot of us are
experiencing chat fatigue.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:47.250
So while we're pivoting over
toward a digital workplace,

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:51.030
at least for now, we found
unexpected opportunities.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.790
While the past few months have
not been easy for most of us,

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:55.680
in some ways it's
become easier to think

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:57.930
beyond the walls of our
office and come together

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.180
as one global team.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.152
Now that you know more about
Women@GoogleCloud, Sherice,

00:09:03.152 --> 00:09:05.610
I want to welcome you to share
more of Google's broader ERG

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:07.450
strategy.

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:08.800
SHERICE TORRES: Thanks, Ruby.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:12.190
I'll start with a brief overview
of the ERG structure at Google

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:15.430
and the role of ERGs in
fostering a sense of community

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:17.560
within underrepresented groups.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:20.500
I'll close with a few case
studies of how organizations

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:24.110
can partner with ERGs to
foster a sense of belonging,

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:26.440
both within
underrepresented communities

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.120
and across the company.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.880
And just a caveat, while
we are excited to share

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:32.950
some of our learnings
with you today, we

00:09:32.950 --> 00:09:35.840
are by no means
experts in this space.

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:39.720
Let's start with an
overview of ERGs at Google.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:42.050
We have 16 Employee
Resource Groups

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:47.070
with over 35,000 members in
50 offices around the world.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.820
In addition to the employees
who volunteer their time

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:52.820
to serve as leaders for our
Employee Resource Groups,

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:55.370
each of our ERGs is
supported by a team

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:59.180
of global and regional
community advisors dedicated

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:01.460
to supporting the
ERGs by driving

00:10:01.460 --> 00:10:04.430
opportunities for
connection, personal,

00:10:04.430 --> 00:10:06.820
and professional development.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:10.480
Community advisors drive
this work in three ways.

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.720
First, they empower ERG
leaders by providing

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:17.790
strategic advisory support
and centralized cross-ERG

00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:20.900
functions, such as
budgeting, governance,

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:24.730
and quarterly Leads Summits
to share best practices.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:28.733
Community advisors help ERG
leaders to recognize and apply

00:10:28.733 --> 00:10:30.150
the leadership
skills that they're

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:32.790
gaining in their community
role and apply them

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.410
to their core jobs.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:38.310
They also form a bridge between
our ERGs, which typically serve

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:40.590
early to mid-level
career Googlers,

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.770
and executive councils
comprised of senior leaders

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:47.520
from each community, serving as
advocates and sponsors for ERG

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:50.610
initiatives, as well as
mentors for more junior members

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:52.800
of each community.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:55.830
Second, community advisors
supplement the programs

00:10:55.830 --> 00:10:58.590
created by local ERG
chapters with programs

00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:02.340
designed to benefit ERG
members around the globe.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:05.580
For example, community
advisors host annual Women

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:08.520
of Color summits,
an ERG buddy program

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:13.050
to assist in onboarding new
employees, allyship workshops,

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:15.570
and pilot sponsorship
programs to support

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:19.620
career progression for
underrepresented communities.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:23.930
Finally, community advisors help
to strengthen Google's business

00:11:23.930 --> 00:11:27.440
by partnering with ERG and
executive council leaders

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:30.080
to infuse the
perspectives and needs

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:33.710
of underrepresented communities
into new and existing Google

00:11:33.710 --> 00:11:38.110
platforms, products,
and policies.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.850
Next, I'll move
into a few examples

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:42.550
of how ERGs help
to foster community

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:45.130
within underrepresented groups.

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:47.065
I'll use Hola as an example.

00:11:47.065 --> 00:11:51.190
Hola is our global ERG focused
on the Hispanic and Latinx

00:11:51.190 --> 00:11:54.520
community, with a mission
to progress inclusion,

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.750
advancement, and representation
of Hispanic, Latinx,

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:01.610
and their allies across Google.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:03.820
Hola fosters community
through a combination

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:07.150
of informal gatherings, such
as First Tuesdays, where

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:09.790
community members gather
for lunch at designated

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:13.690
cafes in each office on the
first Tuesday of each month.

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:17.830
They also have book clubs and
spoken-word open mic nights.

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:21.590
We combine the informal programs
with formal programming,

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:24.400
such as the annual
Hola Leadership Summit,

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:26.920
providing the unique
space for Hola members

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:29.890
to build community,
develop, and better position

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:33.580
each other to lead both
within and outside the context

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:36.670
of the Hispanic and
Latinx community.

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:40.000
The annual Latinas at Google
summit, a two-day event

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:43.930
created for Latinas by
Latinas, with the intention

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:47.590
of building community, providing
networking opportunities,

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:50.110
and developing career
growth, as well

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:53.570
as annual Hispanic Heritage
Month celebrations.

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:56.230
Hola also holds
annual service trips,

00:12:56.230 --> 00:12:59.920
such as their 2019 volunteer
mission to Puerto Rico

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:02.530
to assist the community
in recovery efforts

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:05.370
after Hurricane Maria.

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:08.880
I'd like to close with two
case studies of how community

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:12.750
advisors partner with ERG
leaders and executive councils

00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:15.420
to foster a sense of
belonging, specifically

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:20.000
by supporting underrepresented
communities in times of crisis.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:23.700
My first example relates
to the Asian community.

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:26.610
As many of you may know,
the Asian community,

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:28.580
particularly in
the United States,

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:33.460
experience heightened xenophobia
in the context of COVID-19.

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:36.920
Our community advisors work
hand-in-hand with the leads

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:40.040
from the Asian Google
Network, our ERG dedicated

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:44.060
to supporting the diverse
multicultural Asian community,

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:47.480
and the Asian Leadership
Group, our executive council

00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:50.810
of senior Asian Google leaders,
to provide a combination

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:53.630
of resources to support
the Asian community

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:56.260
in the midst of this crisis.

00:13:56.260 --> 00:14:00.100
First, advisors partnered with
the Asian Leadership Group

00:14:00.100 --> 00:14:03.850
and other Google senior leaders
outside of the Asian community

00:14:03.850 --> 00:14:06.940
to send messages of
acknowledgment, support,

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:10.500
and solidarity to
Asian Googlers.

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:13.830
Next, we paired community
support sessions and wellness

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:16.200
resources for the
Asian community

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:20.780
with a digital AGN COVID-19
community support hub,

00:14:20.780 --> 00:14:25.530
centralizing AGN programming,
resources, and communications

00:14:25.530 --> 00:14:29.130
to support Asian Googlers
through COVID-19 and related

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:32.190
issues of racism and xenophobia.

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:34.620
An AGN giving
campaign was set up

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:38.730
to raise funds for six
US-based nonprofits supporting

00:14:38.730 --> 00:14:41.760
Asian community relief efforts,
with Google matching up

00:14:41.760 --> 00:14:46.260
to $10,000 in
donations per Googler.

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:50.640
Finally, we affirmed, along
with over 100 corporations

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:54.840
and partner organizations, the
COVID-19 Action Agenda penned

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:59.280
by Ascend, the largest
nonprofit pan-Asian organization

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:02.250
for business professionals
in North America.

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:06.600
This combination of internal
support with external advocacy

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:09.330
is helping to create a culture
where Asian Googlers feel

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:15.230
seen, connected, and supported
in the midst of crisis.

00:15:15.230 --> 00:15:18.670
My second example relates
to the Black community.

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:21.580
Over the past several years,
particularly in the summer

00:15:21.580 --> 00:15:24.820
of 2020, violent
and racist attacks

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.250
against the Black community
are forcing the world

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:30.580
to reckon with the structural
and systemic racism Black

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:33.720
people have experienced
over generations.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:37.260
Specifically in the aftermath
of the murder of George Floyd,

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:40.110
community advisors
partnered with global leads

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.930
from the Black Googler
Network, an ERG

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:45.270
designed to cultivate
Black leaders,

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:48.840
empower communities, and
transform technology,

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:51.190
and the Black Leadership
Advisory Group,

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:54.270
our executive council of
senior Black Google leaders,

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:56.340
to step up for the
Black community

00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:57.960
and reaffirm
Google's commitments

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:01.160
to inclusion and racial equity.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:04.790
Similar to our approach
with the Asian community,

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:07.370
we began by acknowledging
the pain of the Black Googler

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:09.950
community through messages
of support from the Black

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:13.100
Leadership Advisory Group,
senior leaders outside

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:16.970
of the Black community, as well
as organic notes of solidarity

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:20.240
from other ERGs and
executive councils.

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:24.230
We're following with a series
of community support, allyship,

00:16:24.230 --> 00:16:26.990
and anti-racism
education resources,

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:29.660
centering first on
supporting Black Googlers

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:34.100
with community-building, mental,
and physical well-being tools,

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:38.030
then following with a series
of company-wide and small group

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:41.360
sessions to assist Googlers at
every stage of their allyship

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:45.420
journey, specifically in the
context of racial equity.

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:49.260
Our internal sessions
focus on bias, privilege,

00:16:49.260 --> 00:16:51.450
and immediate steps
Googlers can take

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:54.750
to show up for their
colleagues, in times of crisis

00:16:54.750 --> 00:16:56.340
and at all times.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:58.440
We followed our
internal programming

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.570
with a dedicated Search
for Racial Equity

00:17:00.570 --> 00:17:03.840
series on the public
Talks at Google platform,

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:08.220
featuring experts in the field
of anti-racism, civil and human

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:09.550
rights.

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:13.310
Most importantly, we went
beyond words to concrete actions

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:16.349
in support of both Black
Googlers and Black communities

00:17:16.349 --> 00:17:20.220
around the world by publicly
committing to build sustainable

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:23.609
equity for Google's Black+
community and to make

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:27.119
our products and programs more
helpful in the moments that

00:17:27.119 --> 00:17:29.310
matter most to Black users.

00:17:29.310 --> 00:17:31.560
You can learn more
about these commitments

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:36.610
at google.com/racial-equity.

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:39.610
I'd like to close our time
today with a definition

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:42.700
from the Center for
Talent Innovation.

00:17:42.700 --> 00:17:45.310
According to the
CTI, organizations

00:17:45.310 --> 00:17:48.130
can foster a sense of
belonging, particularly

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:50.320
among underrepresented
communities,

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:55.360
by creating a culture where all
employees feel seen, connected,

00:17:55.360 --> 00:18:00.200
supported, and proud to be a
part of their organization.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:03.650
As you can see from Ruby's
deep dive into Women@Cloud,

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:06.200
as well as my overview of
Employee Resource Groups

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:09.830
at Google, ERGs are
just one way that we

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:12.650
work to create a culture
of belonging at Google.

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:14.810
Beyond fostering a
sense of community

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:17.420
within underrepresented
groups, ERGs

00:18:17.420 --> 00:18:21.890
can be a powerful tool to
drive advocacy, accountability,

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:24.810
and change across
your organization.

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:26.970
For additional learning,
you can take a look

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:31.060
at the Resources section of
this session on our website.

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:33.920
Thank you so much
for your time today.

